WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
Elon Musk isn’t known to&nbsp;
keep his opinions to himself.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:07.040
If you take Warren Buffett, for example. And to
be totally frank I'm not his biggest fan.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.520
That was on Joe Rogan’s podcast last year.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.760
And later, he cast doubt on Buffett’s public image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.720
when asked by the New York Times&nbsp;
whether Buffett was overrated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.720
saying  that the grandfatherly&nbsp;
figure is “...maybe overstating the case.”

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.000
For Buffett’s part, he’s been a&nbsp;
bit tamer when speaking about Musk.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.209
What do you think of Elon Musk, though? Have you&nbsp;
met him and would you invest in Tesla?&nbsp;

00:00:26.209 --> 00:00:27.120
(Laughter)

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.495
Well, I think you’re trying to bait me a little bit.
Oh no no, I’m not. I’m just asking it. You can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.495 --> 00:00:33.760
say ‘no, no, and no’ or ‘yes, yes, and yes’.
Listen, he’s done some remarkable things.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.040
The two men are similar in the&nbsp;
sense that they’re filthy rich.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.160
Musk’s net worth is $190 billion dollars.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.080
Buffett’s is over $100 billion.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.160
But dig a little deeper and you’ll see they’re&nbsp;
two very different kinds of billionaires.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:47.600
(Applause)

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.520
Warren Buffett is widely considered&nbsp;
the greatest investor of all time.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.200
He’s the Chairman and CEO of Berkshire&nbsp;
Hathaway - a holding company that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.160
consistently given its shareholders&nbsp;
returns of around 20% every year.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.800
That’s without heavily&nbsp;
investing in tech companies.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.106
And would you invest in Tesla?
No.&nbsp;

00:01:05.106 --> 00:01:06.000
Okay.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.960
He’s largely avoided tech&nbsp;
companies because he famously said he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.720
doesn’t understand them well enough so&nbsp;
it’s hard to estimate their future value.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.720
He admitted he messed up by not buying&nbsp;
shares in Google and Amazon years ago.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.960
And instead of picking individual stocks&nbsp;
to buy, Buffett favors index funds like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.080
S &amp; P 500 - a pre-set basket of stocks&nbsp;
that offers more safety and diversity.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.680
Buffett prefers playing it safe&nbsp;
by choosing low-risk investments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.720
His strategy is to buy and hold for decades.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.760
And that’s the fundamental&nbsp;
difference between him and Musk.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.480
Musk is high-risk, high reward with the&nbsp;
potential for very high losses, too.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.840
After making $180 million when the online bank he&nbsp;
ran became PayPal which was then bought by eBay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.400
he sunk all of the profits into three companies.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.360
If things had gone south, Musk&nbsp;
would have been left with nothing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.360
but it was a risk he was willing to take.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.880
Buffett tries to protect his shareholders&nbsp;
from as much risk as possible.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.640
He prefers investing in companies that&nbsp;
have large “moats”, not unlike olden day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.480
castles that had a ditch filled with&nbsp;
water encircling them for protection.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:08.880
In the economic world, a moat protects a company&nbsp;
from the competition by having some sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:14.000
advantage, such as being able to produce at a&nbsp;
lower cost due to cheaper access to raw materials.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.800
Musk doesn’t think this is&nbsp;
the best strategy. He’s said:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.440
“If your only defense against invading&nbsp;
armies is a moat, you will not last long.”

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.560
Instead, “What matters is the pace of innovation…”

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.920
In his case, that means rapidly building&nbsp;
Starships and scaling up the production of Teslas.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.240
In response, Buffett defended the idea of&nbsp;
moats, saying “Elon may turn things upside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.080
down in some areas. I don’t think&nbsp;
he’d want to take us on in candy.”

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.000
Referring to Berkshire Hathaway’s investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.600
in sweets. It owns American&nbsp;
manufacturer See’s Candies.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.920
That prompted some cheeky tweets from Musk:

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.040
“Then I’m going to build a moat&nbsp;
&amp; fill it with candy. Warren B&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.800
will not be able to resist investing!&nbsp;
Berkshire Hathaway kryptonite…”

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.280
And that wasn’t the end of his tweets over sweets:

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.560
“I’m starting a candy company&nbsp;
and it’s going to be amazing”

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.560
“I am super super serious”

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.240
Fair to say Musk is not actually&nbsp;
serious about starting a candy company.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.600
However, he and Buffett are directly&nbsp;
competing against each other.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.600
Berkshire Hathaway is gaining a foothold&nbsp;
in the growing electric vehicle market.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.440
Over a decade ago, Buffett’s business&nbsp;
partner Charlie  Munger convinced him to pick up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.960
an obscure Chinese automaker. BYD - Build&nbsp;
Your Dreams - is a competitor to Tesla.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.880
For his part, Musk is poised to take on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.400
car insurance industry where&nbsp;
Buffett is heavily invested.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.800
In a previous video, I talked about how Tesla&nbsp;
plans to start selling personal car insurance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.200
in more U.S. states with Musk predicting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.560
it could one day account for&nbsp;
a third of Tesla’s business.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.600
That would put Tesla on a collision&nbsp;
course with Berkshire Hathaway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.280
About a third of its business is&nbsp;
in insurance, including GEICO.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.240
Buffett brushes off any sense&nbsp;
of threat, once remarking:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.160
“I’d bet against any company in the&nbsp;
auto business (getting into insurance).”

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.320
On Rogan’s podcast, Musk said he felt&nbsp;
there were two kinds of billionaires who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.160
shouldn’t be seen as one in the same: those&nbsp;
who got rich from engineering and designing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:02.320
a product and those who made an incredible&nbsp;
amount of money playing the stock market.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.080
Though that seems like a harsh assessment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.600
Musk did add that he does see&nbsp;
value in what Buffett does.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.840
He used the example of trying to figure out&nbsp;
whether to invest in Coca-Cola or Pepsi.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.000
Musk said it was “a boring job” to decide which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.440
one deserves more capital but&nbsp;
ultimately an important job.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.760
And speaking of coke, it just happens to be&nbsp;
one of Buffett’s favorite investments and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.800
soft drinks. He downs up to five cans a day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.360
including one that he has every morning&nbsp;
with his $3 and change McDonald’s breakfast.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.840
Buffett is known for living very frugally.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:38.800
The Oracle of Omaha still lives in the same house&nbsp;
in Omaha that he purchased in 1958 for $31,500.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.800
Musk lives well below his means, too.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.480
He’s been known to camp out at Tesla’s&nbsp;
factories when the going has gotten tough.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.240
And his main residence in Boca Chica,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.640
in south Texas is said to be a&nbsp;
house valued at just $50,000.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.560
They also have something else in common. They’ve&nbsp;
pledged to give away most of their fortune.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.640
In the case of Musk, he’s&nbsp;
promised to give away half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.680
on Earth and half on Mars, to build a city there.

